# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
aquinas-second-way.md:1: argue/readings: S4 is read 3 ways: aquinas-second-way.logic.r1 has "order_causes → causes_fi ∧ causes_iu"; aquinas-second-way.logic.r1.2 has "causes_fi ∧ causes_iu"; they differ when only "the first cause is the cause of the intermediate cause" is true, which the first allows; aquinas-second-way.logic.r1.3 has "□(causes_fi ∧ causes_iu)"
aquinas-second-way.md:1: argue/modal-ambiguous: S4 is read aquinas-second-way.logic.r1 and aquinas-second-way.logic.r1.2 with no modality; aquinas-second-way.logic.r1.3 as what is necessary or possible: the text does not say which it means
aquinas-second-way.md:1: argue/answered: S4: answered no, which keeps aquinas-second-way.logic.r1.2 and aquinas-second-way.logic.r1.3, to: Suppose "the first cause is the cause of the intermediate cause" is true and "in the world of sense there is an order of efficient causes", "the intermediate cause is the cause of the ultimate cause" and "necessarily, (the first cause is the cause of the intermediate cause and the intermediate cause is the cause of the ultimate cause)" are false. Is S4, "Now in efficient causes it is not possible to go on to infinity, because in all efficient causes following in order, the first is the cause of the intermediate cause, and the intermediate is the cause of the ultimate cause, whether the intermediate cause be several, or only one.", true then?
aquinas-second-way.md:1: argue/readings: S5 is read 2 ways: aquinas-second-way.logic.r1 and aquinas-second-way.logic.r1.2 have "(causes_fi ∧ ¬first → ¬intermediate) ∧ (causes_iu ∧ ¬intermediate → ¬ultimate)"; aquinas-second-way.logic.r1.3 has "□(causes_fi ∧ ¬first → ¬intermediate) ∧ □(causes_iu ∧ ¬intermediate → ¬ultimate)"; they differ when only "the first cause is the cause of the intermediate cause", "the intermediate cause is the cause of the ultimate cause", "there is a first efficient cause", "there are intermediate efficient causes", "there is an ultimate effect" and "necessarily, (if (the intermediate cause is the cause of the ultimate cause and it is not so that there are intermediate efficient causes), then it is not so that there is an ultimate effect)" are true, which the first allows
aquinas-second-way.md:1: argue/modal-ambiguous: S5 is read aquinas-second-way.logic.r1 and aquinas-second-way.logic.r1.2 with no modality; aquinas-second-way.logic.r1.3 as what is necessary or possible: the text does not say which it means
aquinas-second-way.md:1: argue/answered: S5: answered no, which keeps aquinas-second-way.logic.r1.3, to: Suppose "the first cause is the cause of the intermediate cause", "the intermediate cause is the cause of the ultimate cause", "there is a first efficient cause", "there are intermediate efficient causes", "there is an ultimate effect" and "necessarily, (if (the intermediate cause is the cause of the ultimate cause and it is not so that there are intermediate efficient causes), then it is not so that there is an ultimate effect)" are true and "necessarily, (if (the first cause is the cause of the intermediate cause and it is not so that there is a first efficient cause), then it is not so that there are intermediate efficient causes)" is false. Is S5, "Now to take away the cause is to take away the effect.", true then?
aquinas-second-way.md:1: argue/readings: S7 is read 3 ways: aquinas-second-way.logic.r1 has "regress → ¬first ∧ ¬ultimate ∧ ¬intermediate" and "ultimate ∧ intermediate"; aquinas-second-way.logic.r1.2 has "◇regress → ¬first ∧ ¬ultimate ∧ ¬intermediate" and "ultimate ∧ intermediate"; they differ when only "there is a first efficient cause", "there are intermediate efficient causes", "there is an ultimate effect" and "possibly, in efficient causes one goes on to infinity" are true, which the first allows; aquinas-second-way.logic.r1.3 has "□(regress → ¬first)" and "ultimate ∧ intermediate"
aquinas-second-way.md:1: argue/modal-ambiguous: S7 is read aquinas-second-way.logic.r1 with no modality; aquinas-second-way.logic.r1.2 and aquinas-second-way.logic.r1.3 as what is necessary or possible: the text does not say which it means
aquinas-second-way.md:1: argue/answered: S7: answered no, which keeps aquinas-second-way.logic.r1.2 and aquinas-second-way.logic.r1.3, to: Suppose "there is a first efficient cause", "there are intermediate efficient causes", "there is an ultimate effect" and "possibly, in efficient causes one goes on to infinity" are true and "in efficient causes one goes on to infinity" and "necessarily, (if in efficient causes one goes on to infinity, then it is not so that there is a first efficient cause)" are false. Is S7, "But if in efficient causes it is possible to go on to infinity, there will be no first efficient cause, neither will there be an ultimate effect, nor any intermediate efficient causes; all of which is plainly false.", true then?
aquinas-second-way.md:1: argue/readings: S8 is read 2 ways: aquinas-second-way.logic.r1 and aquinas-second-way.logic.r1.2 have "first → named_god"; aquinas-second-way.logic.r1.3 has "named_god"; they differ when nothing is true, which the first allows
aquinas-second-way.md:1: argue/answered: S8: answered no, which keeps aquinas-second-way.logic.r1.3, to: Suppose "there is a first efficient cause" and "the first efficient cause is what everyone names God" are false. Is S8, "Therefore it is necessary to admit a first efficient cause, to which everyone gives the name of God.", true then? [in 2 of 3 runs]
aquinas-second-way.md:1: argue/negation: aquinas-second-way.logic.r1 P3 (S3): the sentence says "neither", but P3 denies nothing it speaks of: it reads "if a thing is found to be the efficient cause of itself, then a thing would be prior to itself"
aquinas-second-way.md:1: argue/negation: aquinas-second-way.logic.r1 P8 (S7): the sentence says "neither", but P8 denies nothing it speaks of: it reads "there is an ultimate effect and there are intermediate efficient causes"
